WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:03.330
- When most people go into a church,

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.870
they go in there to reflect or to pray.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.860
Most people I think would admit

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.993
that music enhances that experience.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:18.430
There is nothing as moving as the rumbling

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.950
of the lowest pitch pipes on the organ.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.110
The entire room vibrates
with the the sound.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.273
It's a magical feeling.

00:00:30.482 --> 00:00:32.982
(light music)

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.980
OK, we can start.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.230
I'm David Wyld.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.060
I'm the managing director
of Henry Willis and Sons.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.060
We are organ builders, and
we build, restore, repair

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.593
and tune pipe organs all over
the British Isles and beyond.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.770
Henry Willis was born in 1821 in London

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.770
and set up his own company
as an organ builder in 1845.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.050
He built some of the
largest and grandest organs

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.930
of the Victorian period.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.400
Until the Industrial
Revolution, the pipe organ

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.000
was the most complex
mechanism ever devised.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.420
The average pipe organ will have

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.473
up to 10,000 moving parts in it.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:23.430
A pipe organ has pipes
which sound or speak

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.060
when air is blown through the mouth area.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.380
The speaking length of the
pipe determines the pitch.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.830
So, basically, the longer the
pipe, the lower the pitch.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.980
The shorter the pipe,
the higher the pitch.

00:01:38.255 --> 00:01:40.760
The firm has built somewhere
between two-and-a-half

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.760
and 3,000 organs in its
nearly 175 year history.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.090
We still use exactly the same methods;

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.510
some of the equipment is more up-to-date,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.770
but most of the work is hand work.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.960
And we still use many
of the Victorian tools

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.720
that are still in the firm
right from the first days.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.334
The skills involved is, there's casting,

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:08.334
there's the metal forming
and the soldering;

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:10.600
that's just for the pipe making.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.460
There's the joinery skills
required to make wooden pipework.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.543
That's real skill.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.280
When I hear one of our organs
play in a big acoustic,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.760
it can be very moving.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.810
The larger organs have speaking pipes

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:30.030
from a quarter of an inch
long down to 32 feet long

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.440
across an entire tonal
and dynamic spectrum.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.280
And there's no better feeling

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.540
than standing at an opening
recital of a new organ,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.926
looking up at it, and
saying, "We made that."

00:02:42.926 --> 00:02:45.426
(organ music)

